WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.320
A dairy farmer wants her calves fathered by

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:04.480
the best bull in the country, but he

00:00:04.587 --> 00:00:06.160
lives hours away.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.320
A wheat breeder wants one plant with the

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.720
high yield of one variety and the rust

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:12.240
resistance of another.

00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:14.800
Rust is a fungal disease of wheat.

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:16.400
How could each of them use what we

00:00:16.480 --> 00:00:19.280
know about reproduction, and how would you judge

00:00:19.400 --> 00:00:20.800
whether it is worth the cost?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.600
The dairy farmer uses artificial insemination, which means

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:28.960
placing semen, the fluid that carries sperm, into

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.160
a female's reproductive tract without natural mating.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.920
His semen is collected, diluted and split into

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.040
many small doses, and usually frozen in thin

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:41.280
straws that can be sent to any farm.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.040
There, a trained worker thaws one dose and

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.440
uses a thin tube to place it inside

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:48.720
the cow's uterus.

00:00:49.934 --> 00:00:52.093
This works only because of what we know

00:00:52.214 --> 00:00:53.213
about reproduction.

00:00:53.694 --> 00:00:55.613
A cow has an egg ready for only

00:00:55.694 --> 00:00:58.813
a short time in each cycle, around ovulation,

00:00:58.854 --> 00:01:00.333
the release of an egg.

00:01:00.454 --> 00:01:02.013
Farmers call the time when she is ready

00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:03.933
to mate being on heat.

00:01:04.574 --> 00:01:06.653
She releases her egg about a day after

00:01:06.734 --> 00:01:09.213
it starts, so the dose must go in

00:01:09.414 --> 00:01:11.773
during heat, before the egg is released.

00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:14.040
What does it gain?

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:17.400
Calves from a proven bull, one whose merit

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.400
for milk is shown by its daughters' milk

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:23.240
records, on many farms, and faster improvement in

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.800
traits like milk yield than leaving mating to

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:27.080
chance.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:29.560
But the same reach is the risk.

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.320
If a few bulls father most of the

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:34.360
calves, the herd's gene pool, its variety of

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.520
alleles, narrows.

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:38.520
The cows then share more of the same

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:39.720
alleles.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:42.280
If the herd is closely related, the cows

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:44.760
share a weakness to a disease, so one

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.480
disease can spread through the whole herd.

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:49.720
And a harmful allele carried by a popular

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.280
bull can be passed to many calves.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:56.620
Studies of Holstein cows in several countries found

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.500
that as milk yield rose through selection, the

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.260
chance of becoming pregnant after each insemination fell,

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:05.740
and part of the fall was genetic.

00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.220
Insemination makes it easy to use the top

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:11.500
milk bulls everywhere, so selection for one trait

00:02:11.660 --> 00:02:14.700
runs fast, and so does its side effect.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.860
A cow that struggles to get pregnant can

00:02:16.900 --> 00:02:19.020
have a harder life on the farm.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.900
Semen and a trained worker cost money, but

00:02:21.980 --> 00:02:24.220
usually less than buying and keeping a top

00:02:24.300 --> 00:02:25.660
bull on every farm.

00:02:26.867 --> 00:02:30.146
The wheat breeder uses artificial pollination, where a

00:02:30.387 --> 00:02:33.026
person, not the wind or an insect, puts

00:02:33.107 --> 00:02:35.346
pollen on a chosen stigma.

00:02:35.387 --> 00:02:38.866
Wheat flowers usually pollinate themselves, so she first

00:02:38.947 --> 00:02:40.706
removes the anthers from the plant that will

00:02:40.787 --> 00:02:44.146
make the seeds, before they release any pollen.

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:46.866
Then she places pollen from the rust-resistant plant

00:02:46.947 --> 00:02:49.186
on the stigmas and covers the flowers with

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:51.906
a bag, so no other pollen can arrive.

00:02:53.134 --> 00:02:55.453
Pollen is made in the anthers, lands on

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:57.933
a stigma, and a tube carries sperm to

00:02:58.014 --> 00:02:58.813
the egg.

00:02:59.134 --> 00:03:01.693
Every seed has the two parents she picked,

00:03:01.774 --> 00:03:04.253
so her first seedlings all carry alleles from

00:03:04.334 --> 00:03:05.053
both.

00:03:05.374 --> 00:03:07.613
When their seeds grow, the alleles are mixed

00:03:07.694 --> 00:03:09.293
in new ways, as we saw in the

00:03:09.414 --> 00:03:12.173
first video, so only some plants show both

00:03:12.254 --> 00:03:13.213
traits.

00:03:13.294 --> 00:03:15.613
She keeps those and repeats this for several

00:03:15.734 --> 00:03:16.893
generations.

00:03:16.974 --> 00:03:19.293
The gain is a new variety, but it

00:03:19.454 --> 00:03:21.133
takes years of hand work.

00:03:22.334 --> 00:03:25.533
And if farmers everywhere grow that one variety,

00:03:25.654 --> 00:03:27.853
they share the same defences.

00:03:27.934 --> 00:03:30.013
A new strain of the stem rust fungus,

00:03:30.094 --> 00:03:34.093
first found in Uganda in 1998 to 1999,

00:03:34.174 --> 00:03:37.053
overcame a resistance gene that many wheat varieties

00:03:37.174 --> 00:03:37.693
carried.

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:42.020
There is a third option: copy the winner.

00:03:42.340 --> 00:03:44.820
A potato grower can plant pieces of tubers

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:47.940
from one excellent plant, and every new plant

00:03:48.020 --> 00:03:50.100
has the same alleles, so it is a

00:03:50.180 --> 00:03:51.220
clone.

00:03:51.340 --> 00:03:53.860
That gives a uniform crop quickly.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.060
It also means every plant shares one weakness,

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.900
as the Cavendish banana shows.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.860
Evaluate means make a judgement using criteria, which

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:05.380
are the things you judge it by.

00:04:06.020 --> 00:04:08.020
Four make a good start.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:11.860
Gain: what does it improve, shown by evidence

00:04:12.020 --> 00:04:14.260
such as a comparison with similar farms that

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:15.220
do not use it?

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.780
Variety: does it narrow the gene pool?

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:21.700
Cost: money, time and skill.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.820
And for animals, welfare, which is how well

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:25.980
their lives go.

00:04:26.260 --> 00:04:28.820
A good answer gives a judgement, reasons from

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:31.620
each criterion, a weighing, and when it would

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:32.340
change.

00:04:33.567 --> 00:04:36.086
Here is a model answer for dairy farming.

00:04:36.367 --> 00:04:37.966
First, the judgement.

00:04:38.287 --> 00:04:41.006
It is worthwhile when many unrelated bulls are

00:04:41.167 --> 00:04:43.366
used, each on only a share of the

00:04:43.407 --> 00:04:46.206
cows, and health traits count in choosing them.

00:04:46.927 --> 00:04:48.286
Second, the gain.

00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:51.886
Records can compare herds using proven bulls with

00:04:51.967 --> 00:04:54.926
similar herds that mate naturally, and show faster

00:04:55.007 --> 00:04:57.166
gains in traits such as milk yield.

00:04:58.367 --> 00:05:00.326
Third, the variety.

00:05:00.607 --> 00:05:02.366
If a few bulls father most of the

00:05:02.447 --> 00:05:05.646
calves, the gene pool narrows, so one disease

00:05:05.727 --> 00:05:07.406
can spread widely.

00:05:07.607 --> 00:05:10.046
Fourth, cost and welfare.

00:05:10.087 --> 00:05:13.326
Semen and trained workers cost money, and selecting

00:05:13.407 --> 00:05:15.246
only for milk yield has been linked with

00:05:15.327 --> 00:05:17.806
lower fertility, which can be a welfare cost

00:05:17.887 --> 00:05:18.686
for the cow.

00:05:19.900 --> 00:05:21.420
Finally, weigh them.

00:05:21.900 --> 00:05:23.940
Here the gain can be large and the

00:05:23.980 --> 00:05:26.540
money cost is usually lower than keeping top

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.220
bulls on every farm, so the answer turns

00:05:29.260 --> 00:05:32.380
on variety and welfare, and both depend on

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:33.740
how the bulls are chosen.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.340
The verdict: the benefit is real, and it

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:40.700
lasts if many unrelated bulls are used, each

00:05:40.780 --> 00:05:42.940
on only a share of the cows, and

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.860
health traits count in choosing them.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:48.220
Frozen semen, which can be stored for decades,

00:05:48.300 --> 00:05:51.740
from older, different bulls, heritage breeds, which are

00:05:51.860 --> 00:05:54.940
older breeds still bred, and outcrossing, which means

00:05:55.020 --> 00:05:58.700
breeding with unrelated animals, can also protect variety.

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:00.780
Your turn.

00:06:01.020 --> 00:06:02.060
Two situations.

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:05.660
One: a sheep farmer uses frozen semen from

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.060
one champion ram on every ewe in her

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:10.540
flock for ten years, and the farms that

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.740
buy her breeding lambs do the same.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:15.820
Give one benefit of this plan, one risk

00:06:15.860 --> 00:06:17.980
to the flock over those ten years, and

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.460
one change that keeps the benefit and lowers

00:06:20.540 --> 00:06:21.180
that risk.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:25.260
Two: a breeder wants wheat variety P to

00:06:25.420 --> 00:06:28.140
gain rust resistance from variety Q.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:30.940
The flowers of P pollinate themselves.

00:06:31.020 --> 00:06:33.660
She places pollen from Q on P's open

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:36.500
flowers and leaves the plants in the paddock.

00:06:36.620 --> 00:06:39.260
Assume Q's pollen is healthy and every seed

00:06:39.340 --> 00:06:42.620
it fertilises grows into a resistant plant, yet

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.020
many seedlings are not resistant.

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.580
Suggest why, and name two steps she should

00:06:47.820 --> 00:06:48.460
add.

00:06:48.700 --> 00:06:49.820
Pause here to answer.

00:07:00.234 --> 00:07:03.673
One, the benefit is the champion's traits spreading

00:07:03.754 --> 00:07:06.473
quickly across the flock, usually at a lower

00:07:06.634 --> 00:07:09.353
cost than keeping a ram on every farm.

00:07:09.394 --> 00:07:12.393
The strongest risk is a narrow gene pool:

00:07:12.554 --> 00:07:15.593
after ten years most animals share his alleles,

00:07:15.674 --> 00:07:18.313
so one disease, or one harmful allele he

00:07:18.394 --> 00:07:20.553
carries, can reach them all.

00:07:20.714 --> 00:07:23.913
Using semen from several unrelated good rams keeps

00:07:23.994 --> 00:07:26.393
the benefit and lowers that risk.

00:07:26.434 --> 00:07:28.473
A risk that the semen might not work

00:07:28.554 --> 00:07:30.873
is a cost of one dose, not what

00:07:31.034 --> 00:07:32.873
ten years of one ram does to the

00:07:32.914 --> 00:07:33.433
flock.

00:07:34.667 --> 00:07:37.866
Two, some seeds were fertilised by P's own

00:07:37.947 --> 00:07:40.746
pollen, or by pollen blown in, because the

00:07:40.827 --> 00:07:42.906
anthers were still there and the flowers were

00:07:42.987 --> 00:07:43.386
open.

00:07:43.947 --> 00:07:46.586
She should remove P's anthers before they release

00:07:46.667 --> 00:07:48.986
pollen, and cover the flowers with a bag

00:07:49.147 --> 00:07:51.026
after adding Q's pollen.

00:07:51.467 --> 00:07:54.426
If you said Q's pollen failed, the question

00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:56.906
says it is healthy, so the problem is

00:07:56.987 --> 00:07:58.426
which pollen arrived.

00:07:59.634 --> 00:08:02.433
Remember, picking a few parents and using them

00:08:02.514 --> 00:08:05.153
widely speeds up the gain and risks the

00:08:05.314 --> 00:08:05.873
variety.

00:08:06.434 --> 00:08:08.513
So when you see a farming method, ask

00:08:08.674 --> 00:08:13.553
four things: gain, variety, cost, welfare,

00:08:13.634 --> 00:08:15.473
and then say when it is worth it.

00:08:16.514 --> 00:08:19.513
Next time: how a cell copies its DNA,

00:08:19.554 --> 00:08:21.633
the step behind every clone.

00:08:22.834 --> 00:08:24.193
Here are the key notes.

00:08:24.434 --> 00:08:26.273
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:26.354 --> 00:08:27.153
to copy them.
